WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.140
Factories need to catch defects, but
defects are rare.

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.773
Often you have a handful of labelled
examples, sometimes just one.

3
00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:13.405
Can a model learn to spot new defects from
that little?

4
00:00:13.405 --> 00:00:18.038
Anometa puts TabPFN-3.5, a foundation
model for tables, to this test.

5
00:00:19.238 --> 00:00:24.904
The test bed is MVTec AD 2: eight
industrial scenarios, from vials and
walnuts

6
00:00:24.904 --> 00:00:26.928
to sheet metal and fabric.

7
00:00:26.928 --> 00:00:30.975
The training images show only good parts
under regular light.

8
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:35.832
The test images add defects, and lighting
the model has never seen.

9
00:00:37.032 --> 00:00:42.069
To keep the results honest, every
scenario's labelled test images are split
by

10
00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:43.619
scene into two halves.

11
00:00:43.619 --> 00:00:47.107
All experiments and model choices use the
dev half.

12
00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:52.144
The configuration is then frozen, and the
lock half is evaluated exactly once.

13
00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:57.211
Each image goes through a frozen DINOv3
vision transformer.

14
00:00:57.211 --> 00:01:02.797
Its global token and its average patch
become a compact summary, compressed to

15
00:01:02.797 --> 00:01:03.657
sixteen numbers.

16
00:01:03.657 --> 00:01:09.673
Two more numbers measure how far the
image's most unusual patches sit from
their

17
00:01:09.673 --> 00:01:10.962
nearest normal patch.

18
00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:14.829
That gives one row of eighteen numbers per
image.

19
00:01:16.029 --> 00:01:19.044
TabPFN reads a table instead of being
trained.

20
00:01:19.044 --> 00:01:23.945
The context holds about three hundred
normal rows and a few defect rows.

21
00:01:23.945 --> 00:01:28.845
In one forward pass, it predicts the
probability that each new image is

22
00:01:28.845 --> 00:01:29.222
defective.

23
00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:32.237
There are no gradient updates and no
tuning.

24
00:01:33.437 --> 00:01:39.490
On the lock half, with a single labelled
defect, TabPFN reaches an AUROC of zero

25
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:43.928
point seven six, against zero point six
eight for logistic regression.

26
00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:47.560
With five defects it reaches zero point
seven nine.

27
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.805
Its probabilities are also far better
calibrated: half the log loss of logistic

28
00:01:52.805 --> 00:01:53.209
regression.

29
00:01:53.209 --> 00:01:59.261
A good label-free novelty score is just as
strong on ranking, though, so a few

30
00:01:59.261 --> 00:02:00.472
labels add little.

31
00:02:01.672 --> 00:02:04.456
The same idea works at patch level.

32
00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:10.025
Each sixteen by sixteen pixel patch
becomes a row, and TabPFN scores every
patch

33
00:02:10.025 --> 00:02:14.797
to draw a defect map, shown here for one
image per scenario.

34
00:02:14.797 --> 00:02:20.366
On the dev split, averaged over all eight
scenarios, these maps beat the DINOv3

35
00:02:20.366 --> 00:02:21.161
distance map.

36
00:02:21.161 --> 00:02:26.332
They also beat PatchCore, but PatchCore
ran with default settings and was not

37
00:02:26.332 --> 00:02:29.116
tuned, so that gap is not conclusive.

38
00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:35.051
TabPFN needs no training: fitting takes
milliseconds, and one scenario runs end

39
00:02:35.051 --> 00:02:38.998
to end in about fourteen seconds on an RTX
3090.

40
00:02:38.998 --> 00:02:45.706
Results match within a fraction of a point
on an Apple M4 Pro and on a plain

41
00:02:45.706 --> 00:02:46.101
CPU.

42
00:02:46.101 --> 00:02:50.047
Anometa is open source, with every run
recorded and reproducible.
